[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6** (the current stable line at the time of writing).  

It

1. **Creates the map** (you can drop it into any page that already loads the MapLibre script).  
2. **Loads 40 + SVG icons** from your own domain, rasterises them into an `Image` element and registers each one with `map.addImage`.  
3. **Adds the GeoJSON source** that contains ~5 000 venue points, each with a `category` property that matches one of the icon IDs.  
4. **Draws a symbol layer** that uses the `category` property as the `icon-image`.  
5. **Keeps the icons from piling up** when you are zoomed out – the default collision‑detection (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) already does that, but we also add a `symbol-sort-key` so the most‑important venues survive when two icons would collide.

> **TL;DR** – copy the HTML block, replace the placeholder URLs with yours, and you’re done.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 (CDN) -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   CONFIGURATION – change only these two constants
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const ICONS_ROOT = 'https://mydomain.example.com/icons/';   // trailing slash!
const ICONS = [
  // list every icon file name **without** the .svg extension.
  // The string you put here becomes the MapLibre image ID.
  // Example:  "cafe", "park", "museum", …
  'cafe', 'park', 'museum', 'restaurant', 'library',
  // … add the rest of your 40+ icons here …
];
const GEOJSON_URL = 'https://mydomain.example.com/data/venues.geojson';
/* -------------------------------------------------------------- */

(async () => {
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      // a minimal style – you can replace this with any style you like
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: [],
      // IMPORTANT: we *do not* use a sprite here because all icons are
      // added at runtime with addImage().
    },
    center: [-122.42, 37.77], // San Francisco – change as you wish
    zoom: 12,
    hash: true
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Wait for the style to be ready before we start adding images
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  await new Promise(resolve => map.once('style.load', resolve));

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Load every SVG, rasterise it, and register it with addImage()
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // Helper: load a single SVG and return an HTMLImageElement.
  async function loadSvgAsImage(id, url) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const img = new Image(24, 24);          // you can change the size here
      img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';         // required for cross‑origin SVGs
      img.onload = () => resolve(img);
      img.onerror = e => reject(new Error(`SVG "${id}" failed to load: ${e}`));
      img.src = url;
    });
  }

  // Load *all* icons in parallel – MapLibre will reject duplicate IDs,
  // so we guard with hasImage().
  await Promise.all(ICONS.map(async id => {
    const url = `${ICONS_ROOT}${id}.svg`;
    const img = await loadSvgAsImage(id, url);
    if (!map.hasImage(id)) {
      // `sdf: false` (default) – we keep the original colours of the SVG.
      map.addImage(id, img, { sdf: false });
    }
  }));

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Add the GeoJSON source (5 000 venues)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: GEOJSON_URL,
    // optional: you can enable clustering if you want a different visual
    // approach at very low zooms.  For pure icons we keep it flat.
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Add the symbol layer that draws the icons
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    // The icon name comes directly from the feature property `category`.
    // It must match one of the IDs we added above.
    layout: {
      // 5 000 points is fine – the default collision detection will hide
      // icons that would overlap at the current zoom.
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],
      // Optional: make the icons a little smaller on the map.
      'icon-size': 0.8,

      // When two icons collide, the one with the *higher* sort‑key wins.
      // Here we use the feature's `rank` property (if you have one) or
      // fall back to the zoom level – higher zoom = higher priority.
      // If you have no ranking, you can omit this line.
      'symbol-sort-key': ['coalesce', ['get', 'rank'], ['zoom']],

      // Keep the default: icons are not allowed to overlap.
      // (You could set `icon-allow-overlap: true` for a “show‑everything”
      // mode, but that defeats the “no pile‑up” requirement.)
      'icon-allow-overlap': false,
      // If you ever want the text label to appear even when the icon is
      // hidden, set `icon-optional: true`.  Not needed here because we
      // have no text field.
    },
    paint: {
      // If you ever switch to SDF icons you could colour them here.
      // 'icon-color': '#ff6600',
    }
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣  Helpful debugging – log any missing images
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('styleimagemissing', e => {
    console.warn(`⚠️  Image "${e.id}" is missing from the sprite/runtime images.`);
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 7️⃣  (Optional) Fit the map to the data once it loads
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  map.once('data', e => {
    if (e.sourceId === 'venues' && e.isSourceLoaded) {
      const bounds = new maplibregl.LngLatBounds();
      const source = map.getSource('venues');
      // source._data is the raw GeoJSON (only after it has been fetched)
      source._data.features.forEach(f => bounds.extend(f.geometry.coordinates));
      map.fitBounds(bounds, { padding: 40, maxZoom: 15 });
    }
  });

})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 2️⃣  Why this works

| Step | What it does | Why it matters for your use‑case |
|------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| **`map.once('style.load')`** | Guarantees the style (even an empty one) is ready before we call `addImage`. | `addImage` throws if the style isn’t loaded yet. |
| **`loadSvgAsImage`** | Creates an `HTMLImageElement` from the SVG URL (the browser rasterises the vector). | MapLibre can only work with raster images; this lets you keep your source files as SVGs. |
| **`map.addImage(id, img, {sdf:false})`** | Registers the rasterised image under the same ID you’ll use in the data (`category`). | The `icon-image` property can now reference any of the 40+ IDs. |
| **`icon-image: ['get','category']`** | Uses a *data‑driven* value – each feature picks the icon that matches its `category`. | No need for 5 000 separate layers; one layer handles all categories. |
| **Collision detection (default)** | `icon-allow-overlap` defaults to `false`, so MapLibre hides icons that would overlap at the current zoom. | Prevents the “pile‑up” you asked for when zoomed out. |
| **`symbol-sort-key` (optional)** | Gives you deterministic control over which icon survives a collision (higher value wins). | If you have a `rank` or similar priority field you can make important venues stay visible. |
| **`styleimagemissing` listener** | Prints a warning for any `category` value that does **not** have a matching runtime image. | Makes debugging missing icons trivial. |
| **CORS (`img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous'`)** | Required when the SVGs are hosted on a different origin than the page. | Without it `addImage` would throw a security error. |

---

## 3️⃣  Things you may want to tweak

| What | How |
|------|-----|
| **Icon size** | Change `'icon-size': 0.8` to any number (1 = original 24 px). |
| **Different rasterisation dimensions** | Adjust `new Image(24,24)` to the pixel size you prefer; the SVG will be scaled to that size. |
| **Clustering** | If you want a cluster‑based approach at very low zooms, add `cluster: true` to the source and a separate layer for clusters. |
| **SDF icons** | If you ever need colour‑theming (`icon-color`, `icon-halo-*`), generate SDF PNGs (or use `spritezero` with `--sdf`) and register them with `{sdf:true}`. |
| **Sprite instead of runtime images** | For a *static* set of icons you could pre‑build a sprite sheet (PNG + JSON) and point the style’s `sprite` property at it – this reduces the number of HTTP requests. |
| **Custom fonts for labels** | If you later add a `text-field` (e.g., the venue name), make sure the style’s `glyphs` URL points at a font server (or use the default OpenMapTiles glyphs). |

---

## 4️⃣  Full‑stack checklist

1. **Host the SVGs** on a domain that serves them with **CORS headers** (`Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` or your site’s origin).  
2. **Make sure the GeoJSON** (`venues.geojson`) contains a `category` property whose value exactly matches one of the IDs in the `ICONS` array (case‑sensitive).  
3. **Deploy the HTML** (or embed the script in your existing page).  
4. **Open the page** – you should see the map, the icons, and no pile‑up when you zoom out.  
5. **Open the browser console** – any missing icons will be listed as warnings.

That’s it! 🎉 You now have a performant, data‑driven icon layer for 5 000 venues, using your own SVG assets, with built‑in collision handling. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + category icons are stored as **SVG files** on your own domain (`https://my‑site.com/icons/<name>.svg`).  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains ~5 000 point features, each with a string property `category` that matches one of the SVG file names (e.g. `"cafe"`, `"park"` …).  
* The icons are **rasterised at run‑time** with `Map.addImage()`.  
* Collision handling is left to the style engine (`icon‑allow‑overlap: false` – the default) so that, when you are zoomed out, icons that would overlap are automatically hidden.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS v6 CSS & JS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Configuration – change these to match your own setup
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN = '';               // not needed for pure MapLibre, keep empty
const MAP_STYLE = 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json'; // any style you like
const ICON_BASE_URL = 'https://my-site.com/icons/'; // trailing slash
const ICON_NAMES = [
  // list every category name that exists as an SVG file
  'cafe', 'park', 'restaurant', 'library', 'museum', 'school',
  'hospital', 'bank', 'pharmacy', 'post_office', 'hotel', 'bar',
  // … add the rest (≈40 total) …
];
const GEOJSON_URL = 'https://my-site.com/data/venues.geojson';
/* -------------------------------------------------------------- */

(async () => {
  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     3️⃣  Initialise the map
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: MAP_STYLE,
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],   // San Francisco – change as you wish
    zoom: 12,
    // optional: set maxZoom/minZoom if you want tighter control
  });

  // Wait for the style to be fully loaded before we start adding images
  await new Promise(resolve => map.once('load', resolve));

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     4️⃣  Helper – load an SVG, rasterise it into an Image object
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  function loadSvgAsImage(url, size = 24) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const img = new Image(size, size);
      img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';          // required for cross‑origin SVGs
      img.onload = () => resolve(img);
      img.onerror = e => reject(new Error(`Failed to load SVG ${url}`));
      img.src = url;
    });
  }

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     5️⃣  Load *all* SVG icons and register them with the map
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  const iconPromises = ICON_NAMES.map(async name => {
    const url = `${ICON_BASE_URL}${name}.svg`;
    const img = await loadSvgAsImage(url, 24);   // 24 px square – adjust if you like
    // Guard against duplicate IDs (e.g. if the style already ships a “cafe” icon)
    if (!map.hasImage(name)) {
      map.addImage(name, img, { sdf: false });   // ordinary raster image
    }
  });

  // Wait until every icon is in the style
  await Promise.all(iconPromises);
  console.log('All icons loaded and added to the style');

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     6️⃣  Add the GeoJSON source (5 000 points)
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: GEOJSON_URL,
    // optional: enable clustering if you want a different visual at very low zooms
    // cluster: true,
    // clusterRadius: 50
  });

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     7️⃣  Add the symbol layer that uses the icons
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    // The icon name comes directly from the feature property `category`
    layout: {
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],   // e.g. "cafe", "park", …
      'icon-size': 1,                      // scale factor – tweak if icons look too big/small
      // Collision handling (default is false → icons hide on overlap)
      // You can fine‑tune the priority with `symbol-sort-key` if you have a
      // secondary ranking (e.g. popularity). Here we just let the engine decide.
      // 'symbol-sort-key': ['get', 'rank']   // optional
    },
    paint: {
      // Optional visual tweaks
      // 'icon-color': '#2a2a2a',   // works only on SDF icons – ignored for normal SVGs
      // 'icon-opacity': 0.9
    }
  });

  /* --------------------------------------------------------------
     8️⃣  (Optional) Show a popup with the venue name on click
     -------------------------------------------------------------- */
  const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ closeButton: false, closeOnClick: false });

  map.on('click', 'venue-icons', e => {
    const feature = e.features[0];
    const coordinates = feature.geometry.coordinates.slice();
    const name = feature.properties.name || 'Unnamed venue';
    const category = feature.properties.category;

    popup.setLngLat(coordinates)
         .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>Category: ${category}`)
         .addTo(map);
  });

  // Change the cursor to a pointer when hovering a venue
  map.on('mouseenter', 'venue-icons', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
  map.on('mouseleave', 'venue-icons', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');

})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## How the pieces fit together

| Step | What happens | Why it matters for your use‑case |
|------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| **3️⃣ Initialise the map** | `map.once('load')` guarantees the style (including any default sprite) is ready before we start adding our own images. | Prevents the “styleimagemissing” warning that would appear if we tried to add images too early. |
| **4️⃣ loadSvgAsImage()** | Creates an `HTMLImageElement` from the remote SVG (cross‑origin enabled) and resolves when the rasterisation finishes. | `Map.addImage()` only accepts raster images (PNG/WebP/JPEG) or an `Image` element; this is the bridge for SVGs. |
| **5️⃣ addImage() for every icon** | Each SVG is registered under the exact string that appears in the GeoJSON `category` property. `hasImage()` avoids duplicate‑ID errors if the base style already ships an icon with the same name. | The style now knows about **all 40+ icons**; the layer can reference them directly via `['get','category']`. |
| **6️⃣ GeoJSON source** | Loads the 5 000 venue points in one request (or you could inline the data). | Keeps the data separate from the style, allowing you to update it without rebuilding the sprite. |
| **7️⃣ symbol layer** | `icon-image` is an **expression** that reads the feature’s `category`. Collision handling is left at the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`), so when you zoom out the engine hides overlapping icons automatically. | Exactly what you asked for: each venue shows its own icon, and the icons “don’t pile up” at low zooms. |
| **8️⃣ optional popup** | Demonstrates how to interact with the points; not required for the core task. | Shows that the icons are still regular MapLibre features you can query. |

---

## Tweaking collision behaviour (optional)

If you find that too many icons disappear when you are moderately zoomed out, you can:

```js
layout: {
  'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],
  'icon-allow-overlap': true,          // draw every icon, ignore collisions
  // or:
  'icon-ignore-placement': true,       // same effect, newer name
  // or give priority to certain venues:
  'symbol-sort-key': ['get', 'popularity']   // higher value → drawn on top
}
```

But **the default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) already does exactly what you described** – hide icons that would overlap, keeping the map readable.

---

## Things to double‑check in your own deployment

1. **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or at least your map domain). Otherwise `new Image()` will throw a security error.
2. **SVG size** – The `new Image(width, height)` call determines the rasterised pixel size. If you need different sizes per category, you can store that information in a lookup table and call `addImage(name, img, { pixelRatio: … })` accordingly.
3. **Cache** – Browsers will cache the SVGs automatically; the `Promise.all()` pattern ensures we only request each file once.
4. **Performance** – 5 000 points is well within the limits of a single symbol layer. If you ever go to > 50 000 points, consider **clustering** (`cluster: true` in the source) or **vector‑tile** sources.

That’s it! Drop the HTML into a file, adjust the three configuration constants (`ICON_BASE_URL`, `ICON_NAMES`, `GEOJSON_URL`), and you’ll have a fully functional MapLibre map with 5 000 venues each showing its own SVG‑derived icon, with automatic collision avoidance. Happy mapping!

## TL;DR  

*The current rule*  

```js
if (layer.type === 'symbol' &&
    layer.layout?.['symbol-placement'] === 'point' &&   // default = point
    layer.layout?.['text‑anchor'] === undefined) {      // default = "center"
    // → “shield”
}
```

*Why it mis‑fires*  

| What the rule looks for | What many **non‑shield** layers also have | What a real shield layer usually has |
|--------------------------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| `symbol‑placement: 'point'` (or omitted) | POI / place‑name point layers, city‑label points, custom markers, etc. | **Usually** `symbol‑placement` is *data‑driven* (`'point'` for point sources, `'line'` for line sources) **or omitted** – the same as any point symbol. |
| `text‑anchor` missing (defaults to `"center"`) | Same default for *any* symbol layer that has a text field. | Shields also default to `"center"` – the property is rarely set explicitly. |
| No other checks | → **All** of the above are true for the majority of point‑symbol layers, so the heuristic tags them as shields. | Shields have **additional, distinctive clues** that the rule ignores (icon‑image expression, text‑field expression, badge‑size logic, etc.). |

In short: **the heuristic is not sound** because it only checks two *default* values that are shared by almost every point‑symbol layer. It will flag ordinary place‑name or POI layers as shields and will miss shields whose layout deviates from the defaults (e.g., a shield that explicitly sets `text‑anchor: "center"` or that lives on a line source).

---

## What actually distinguishes a *route‑shield* layer?

A shield is a **symbol layer that combines two things**:

1. **A badge image** (the “shield” background) taken from the sprite sheet.  
   - The `icon-image` value is **dynamic** – it contains placeholders that are replaced per‑feature, e.g.  
     ```json
     "icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}"
     // or
     "icon-image": "road_{ref_length}"
     // or a prefixed version: "my-icons:us-interstate_{ref_length}"
     ```
   - The placeholder names are **`network`, `ref`, `ref_length`** (or sometimes just `ref_length`).  
   - The resulting IDs end with an underscore and a number (`_1`, `_2`, … `_6`) that matches the length of the road number.

2. **A text label** that shows the actual road number.  
   - `text-field` is an expression that pulls the road reference from the feature, e.g. `"{ref}"` or `["get","ref"]`.  
   - The layer almost always has a `text-font` (often `["Open Sans Regular","Arial Unicode MS Regular"]`) and a `text-size` that is tuned for shields.  
   - The text is **centered** on the badge (`text-anchor: "center"` – the default) and usually **does not** have a `text-optional` flag.

Additional clues that are *optional* but common in the official OSM Bright / OSM Liberty styles:

| Property | Typical shield value | Why it helps |
|----------|---------------------|--------------|
| `icon-text-fit` | `"both"` **or** `"width"` (only for *stretchable* shields) | Indicates the badge is meant to grow/shrink to fit the text. |
| `icon-text-fit-padding` | `[2, 2, 2, 2]` (or similar) | Present only on stretchable shields. |
| `icon-size` | Often a constant like `0.8` or a zoom‑function | Not unique, but combined with the above it narrows the set. |
| `icon-allow-overlap` / `icon-overlap` | Usually `true` (shields are drawn on top of roads) | Again, not unique, but useful when combined. |
| `source-layer` name | `"transportation_name"` (OpenMapTiles) or `"road_shield"` (custom) | Naming convention can be a quick filter. |
| `filter` | Often `["has","ref"]` or `["==",["get","network"],"us-interstate"]` | Shields are only drawn when a reference exists. |

**What you *won’t* see on a plain place‑name layer**

* No placeholder in `icon-image` (it will be a static name like `"city"` or `"cafe"`).  
* No `text-field` that references `{ref}` (it will be something like `"{name}"`).  
* No `icon-text-fit` / `icon-text-fit-padding`.  
* No `ref_length`‑style IDs in the sprite sheet.

---

## A more reliable heuristic

Below is a **compact, production‑ready** function that you can drop into a style‑audit script. It tries to be tolerant of the many ways a shield can be expressed while staying strict enough to avoid false positives.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route‑shield layer.
 *
 * @param {Object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style.
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer with an icon and a text field.
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;
  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // icon-image must exist (shields always have a badge)
  if (!layout['icon-image']) return false;

  // text-field must exist and reference a road reference.
  // Accept both the classic "{ref}" string and an expression that gets "ref".
  const tf = layout['text-field'];
  const hasRefText =
    typeof tf === 'string' && /\{ref\}/.test(tf) ||
    Array.isArray(tf) && (
      // ["get","ref"]  or  ["format",["get","ref"],...]
      tf[0] === 'get' && tf[1] === 'ref' ||
      tf[0] === 'format' && tf.some(v => Array.isArray(v) && v[0] === 'get' && v[1] === 'ref')
    );

  if (!hasRefText) return false;

  // 2️⃣ Look for the *placeholder* pattern in icon-image.
  //   Accept:
  //   - "{network}_{ref_length}"
  //   - "road_{ref_length}"
  //   - "us-interstate_{ref_length}"
  //   - prefixed forms "my-icons:us-interstate_{ref_length}"
  const iconImg = layout['icon-image'];
  const placeholderPattern = /(?:\{[^}]+\}|[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+)_{ref_length}$/;
  if (!placeholderPattern.test(iconImg)) return false;

  // 3️⃣ Optional but strong signals (if present, they must match):
  //    a) icon-text-fit is not "none"
  //    b) icon-text-fit-padding exists
  //    c) filter checks for a "ref" property
  if (layout['icon-text-fit'] && layout['icon-text-fit'] !== 'none') {
    // ok – keep going
  } else if (layout['icon-text-fit'] === undefined) {
    // fine – many shields omit it (static badge per length)
  } else {
    return false;
  }

  // If a filter exists, make sure it references "ref" or "network"
  if (Array.isArray(layer.filter)) {
    const filterStr = JSON.stringify(layer.filter);
    if (!/ref|network/.test(filterStr)) return false;
  }

  // 4️⃣ Finally, sanity‑check that the sprite sheet actually contains
  //    at least one of the expected badge IDs.
  //    (Only possible when you have the sprite JSON handy; otherwise skip.)
  //    Example:
  //    if (spriteIndex && !Object.keys(spriteIndex).some(k => /_1$/.test(k))) return false;

  return true;
}
```

### Why this works

| Step | What it catches | Why it eliminates false positives |
|------|----------------|-----------------------------------|
| **1️⃣ Icon + text** | Shields always have a badge **and** a road number label. | Plain POI layers either have no `icon-image` (text‑only) or have a static `icon-image` (no dynamic badge). |
| **2️⃣ Placeholder in `icon-image`** | Looks for the `{…}_{ref_length}` pattern that is the hallmark of the OSM‑style shields. | Place‑name layers use static names (`"city"`, `"town"`), not placeholders. |
| **3️⃣ Optional “fit” / filter clues** | If the layer uses stretchable badges (`icon-text-fit` ≠ `none`) we verify it; otherwise we ignore it. | Keeps the function tolerant of both static‑badge shields (most OSM Bright/Liberty) and stretchable‑badge shields (custom styles). |
| **4️⃣ Sprite‑index sanity check** (optional) | Guarantees the referenced badge IDs actually exist in the loaded sprite. | Prevents a layer that *looks* like a shield but points at a missing sprite from being mis‑identified. |

You can **tune** the heuristic further:

* Add a check for `layout['text-font']` containing a known shield font (e.g., `"Open Sans Bold"`).  
* Require `layout['icon-size']` to be a number or a zoom‑function (shields rarely use `icon-size: 0`).  
* If you have the source‑layer name, treat `"transportation_name"` or `"road_shield"` as a strong positive signal.

